{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Write for yourself these words, for according to the mouth of these words I have made a covenant with you.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'write for yourself' means that he should write the conditions between the Holy One, blessed be He, and Israel, and how they accepted the Torah with 'we will do and we will hear,' just as they did with the first tablets; and He commanded Moses to make of this a book. And that He went back and mentioned 'for according to the mouth of these words' a second time — when He could have said 'according to their mouth' — our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted this as: words that I have delivered to you in writing, you are not permitted to recite by mouth; and words that I have delivered to you by mouth, you are not permitted to write them. And this will be the meaning of the verse: if you have fulfilled these words — what is in writing, in writing, and what is oral, orally — I have made a covenant with you; and if you altered this, I have not made a covenant with you." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> R. Yehudah bar Shalom said: Moses our master, peace be upon him, asked that the Mishnah too be in writing. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: 'I would write for you the majority of My teaching' (a play on Numbers 30:14) — but they would be accounted as a stranger's (הושיענו אל אמתך, etc.). And why all this? Because the Mishnah constitutes the mysteries of the Holy One, blessed be He, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not reveal His mysteries except to those who fear Him, as it is said (Psalms 25:14): 'סוד ה׳ ליראיו' — 'the secret of HaShem is to those who fear Him.' End quote." "<b>And it appears to me</b> in His saying that the Mishnah is 'mysteries' — that the Mishnah reveals mysteries, while the written Torah is the mysteries themselves — once you already know that the Mishnah corresponds to the second ה in the Name, which is the explanation of the Name that is hidden and concealed, and because of it the Name is called 'the Explicit Name' (השם המפורש); so too the Mishnah is the explanation of the written Torah, and the written Torah is revealed and explained through it. And for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted it to be written on the tablet of the heart and did not grant permission to Moses to write it, for in this there would be a revelation of mysteries, and the other nations who did not accept the Torah and did not desire it would become equal with Israel. And this is what He said: 'they would be accounted as a stranger's' — that all would be equal in its writing, and the acceptors of the Torah would have no advantage over them. And there is yet another reason: so that the idolaters would not have an opening of the mouth on the day of judgment. And I will draw you a parable — to what may the matter be likened? To a king who sent a signed letter to his sons by the hand of his servant, the faithful one of his household, and commanded them in that letter how they should conduct themselves toward him and toward the people of the world; and he entrusted to the servant things orally, which he would tell them as the completion of the words of the letter. And the king fixed a great reward for all who would enter into his covenant and fulfill the words of his letter according to his command. What did the people of the city do? They copied the letter and said: 'We too have the king's letter in our hands, and we fulfill it.' When the time came for the king to give reward, the people of the city came and stood before him, and he asked whether they had engaged in the words of his letter to do according to his command; and since they did not know those oral matters, he said: 'Their souls be damned.'" "<b>And for this</b> they hinted in the first chapter of Avodah Zarah: In the time to come, the Holy One, blessed be He, brings a Torah scroll and lays it in His lap and says: 'Let him who has dealt in it come and take his reward,' as it is said (Isaiah 43:9): 'מי בהם יגיד זאת' — and 'this' (זאת) is nothing but the Torah, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:44): 'וזאת התורה' — and immediately they go forth from before Him in dejection of soul." "<b>And our master</b> the Holy One (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi), who composed the Mishnah and taught it publicly, and everyone wrote it down in his lifetime — his intention was so that Torah would not be forgotten from Israel, for he saw wickedness spreading in the world and Israel scattered in exile; therefore he was permitted to do so, as it is said (Psalms 119:126): 'עת לעשות לה׳ הפרו תורתך' — 'it is a time to act for HaShem; they have voided Your Torah.' And he wrote and composed the Mishnah, which is the Oral Torah, and therefore called it 'Mishnah,' because it is second (שניה) to the written Torah, and most of it is in pure Hebrew language like the written Torah. And afterward wisdom diminished and hearts grew short, and Ravina and Rav Ashi arose and composed the Talmud, which is the explanation of the Mishnah — for in the greatness of the wisdom of our holy master and the wisdom of the men of his generation, the explanation of the Torah was, in their view, clear and plain from within the Mishnah itself; but for the generations of Ravina and Rav Ashi it had become very deep and obscure. And from this they said in the Talmud concerning the Mishnah: חסורי מחסרא והכי קתני — 'it is missing what is missing, and thus it teaches' — and the intention is not that the Mishnah is lacking at all, heaven forbid, but the intention is that it is lacking for us, because of the deficiency of our intellect, because we do not reach the depth of the wisdom of the generation of the sages of the Mishnah. Therefore Ravina and Rav Ashi made an explanation upon the Mishnah, which is the Talmud, and they clarified the difficulties of the Mishnah and extended their language upon the language of the Mishnah, which was deep and obscure.<br> <b>And</b> what it says, 'with you' (אתך) — that is, on your account, for in his merit He made it with them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:18.590741120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}